why 6 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The D chord carries over the bar line while soprano, alto and bass move in passing eighths. In this flowing 3/4 the decorations, not chord changes, keep the music moving.
why 6 1/2 CROSSING The tenor steps down A–G–F♯ while the bass leaps up to B and walks back down A–G–E. For a beat the bass is above the tenor; both lines keep their own shape.
why 6 1/2 DOUBLING Only the crossing makes this a ⁶₄. With Bach's bass on B it is a B minor chord (vi) in root position, and B is doubled correctly.
why 6 1/2 SIX_FOUR The bass leaps above the tenor, so the tenor's F♯ is the lowest note and vi sounds as a ⁶₄. Bach's continuo doubled the bass an octave lower, so B stayed at the bottom in his performance.
why 6 5/8 CROSSING The same crossing: the bass passes A on its way down to G and E, and by the next beat it is below the tenor again.
why 12 0 LEAPS The bass rises F♯–B–E in fourths: roots moving by fifths (V of ii, then ii), the strongest bass motion there is. The fermata ends the line there.
why 14 3/8 CROSSING The tenor walks down G–F♯–E into the bass's register while the bass climbs E–F♯–G, then jumps back up an octave. Both lines keep moving by step.
why 14 1/2 CROSSING The tenor leaps up an octave to E just as the alto drops to B, so they trade places for a beat. Then the alto leaps back up to G.
why 14 5/8 CROSSING The swap goes on: the tenor steps down E–D–C♯ while the alto waits on B; on the downbeat the alto is above it again.
why 17 3/8 CROSSING The tenor leaps down to F♯ while the bass walks down B–A–G–F♯. For an eighth the bass is above it, then the tenor climbs back as the bass reaches F♯.
why 20 1/4 DOUBLING The soprano holds B while the tenor climbs G–A–B by step toward C♯ and D. The tenor's line matters more than the doubling.
why 20 5/8 MELODIC The bass drops a seventh, B to C♯, instead of rising a step, to stay low for the cadence. It is the step B–C♯ moved down an octave, and the bass goes on by step to D.
why 20 5/8 OVERLAP The bass has just leapt far down, so the tenor's A lies below where the bass was a moment ago. With the bass now an octave lower, the lines stay clear.
why 21 0 PROGRESSION The chord over C♯ is really a dominant, A–C♯–G, with the alto's D held over from before. Read that way it is V⁶₅ resolving to I, as expected.
